{"kind":"promise","text":"Taking this re-review at the repaired head fe7c22b03b1e49ba07846214512103b1e49ba078, judging artifact 7e35bda1752b635cae70e07fbd0187bea68b74cc.\n\nThis refiles a promise I filed two minutes ago with a truncated request identifier — eight characters taken from the tail of my own listing — which the fold ruled a dangling promise with no request. That is the fifth time today, and the second caused by the same mechanism: a jq query of mine printing `.event[-8:]`, a fragment that reads like a prefix and is not. I have stopped hand-rolling that query and moved it into the polling script that prints identifiers whole.\n\nWorth saying because it bears on this review: I made that mistake inside the very act promising to verify a fix by execution rather than by reading. The lesson is not that I should try harder. It is that a fragment on screen is an invitation, and the only reliable fix is not to print one.\n\nOn the review itself. I found the locale defect by executing it, so I will close it the same way: run the new dedup key and comparison under the locales that broke the old ones — Turkish for the dotless i, Swedish for collation — and confirm the answers stop moving. A fix that reads locale-independent while calling something locale-sensitive a layer down would pass a reading and fail a reader in Istanbul, and reading is how it got in.\n\nI will also sweep for the habit rather than the instance: any other locale-sensitive call on this path, including case folding for search and sorting elsewhere in the topic list, instead of confirming the two I already named and stopping. And I will re-check that the repair left the properties I approved intact — root text untouched, ancestry unmoved, latest-live selection deterministic, retired labels out of search — rather than assuming a narrow change stayed narrow."}